    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(4))  # F0..F3\n    customers = list(range(15))  # C0..C14\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(25, 43), (24, 53), (14, 53), (27, 67)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(32, 10), (64, 93), (8, 23), (47, 30), (98, 71), (72, 58), (18, 15), (28, 56), (69, 25), (94, 96), (25, 98), (60, 94), (21, 14), (74, 66), (37, 86)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [226.0, 174.0, 195.0, 134.0]\n    customer_demands = [42.0, 23.0, 35.0, 46.0, 48.0, 50.0, 32.0, 34.0, 45.0, 44.0, 24.0, 11.0, 18.0, 50.0, 11.0]\n    fixed_costs = [343.0, 349.0, 159.0, 202.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse opening and fulfillment plan to minimize total cost\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at the point where we need to choose which of our candidate warehouse sites to activate and how to serve our customer base from those sites at the lowest overall cost. We have four potential locations under consideration and fifteen customer delivery points across the region.\n\nThe candidate warehouse locations are provided in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (25, 43)\n  - F1: (24, 53)\n  - F2: (14, 53)\n  - F3: (27, 67), and the customer locations are provided in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (32, 10)\n  - C1: (64, 93)\n  - C2: (8, 23)\n  - C3: (47, 30)\n  - C4: (98, 71)\n  - C5: (72, 58)\n  - C6: (18, 15)\n  - C7: (28, 56)\n  - C8: (69, 25)\n  - C9: (94, 96)\n  - C10: (25, 98)\n  - C11: (60, 94)\n  - C12: (21, 14)\n  - C13: (74, 66)\n  - C14: (37, 86). Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates. This ensures we\u2019re comparing true point\u2011to\u2011point proximity when we estimate transportation effort.\n\nOpening any warehouse entails a one-time commitment, captured in [343.0, 349.0, 159.0, 202.0]. Once a facility is open, shipping costs scale with both the distance traveled and the volume moved, based on the transport rate in 0.5. In other words, moving more units or moving them farther will increase cost accordingly.\n\nOperationally, each potential facility has a finite throughput it can support, summarized in [226.0, 174.0, 195.0, 134.0]. On the demand side, every customer has a required volume that must be met, as listed in [42.0, 23.0, 35.0, 46.0, 48.0, 50.0, 32.0, 34.0, 45.0, 44.0, 24.0, 11.0, 18.0, 50.0, 11.0]. A facility can only ship if it is opened, and the total volume shipped from any open facility cannot exceed its capacity. Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied in the final plan. The shipment plan will specify how much to send from each open warehouse to each customer to meet these commitments.\n\nOur objective is straightforward: minimize total cost by balancing where we pay to open facilities and how we allocate shipments across the network. The outcome of this analysis will be a recommended set of warehouses to open, the allocation of customer demand to those open sites, the expected utilization of each facility, and a clear view of the resulting transportation spend and overall network cost.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse opening and fulfillment plan to minimize total cost\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at the point where we need to choose which of our candidate warehouse sites to activate and how to serve our customer base from those sites at the lowest overall cost. We have four potential locations under consideration and fifteen customer delivery points across the region.\n\nThe candidate warehouse locations are provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and the customer locations are provided in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates. This ensures we\u2019re comparing true point\u2011to\u2011point proximity when we estimate transportation effort.\n\nOpening any warehouse entails a one-time commitment, captured in {FIXED_COSTS}. Once a facility is open, shipping costs scale with both the distance traveled and the volume moved, based on the transport rate in {TRANSPORT_RATE}. In other words, moving more units or moving them farther will increase cost accordingly.\n\nOperationally, each potential facility has a finite throughput it can support, summarized in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}. On the demand side, every customer has a required volume that must be met, as listed in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}. A facility can only ship if it is opened, and the total volume shipped from any open facility cannot exceed its capacity. Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied in the final plan. The shipment plan will specify how much to send from each open warehouse to each customer to meet these commitments.\n\nOur objective is straightforward: minimize total cost by balancing where we pay to open facilities and how we allocate shipments across the network. The outcome of this analysis will be a recommended set of warehouses to open, the allocation of customer demand to those open sites, the expected utilization of each facility, and a clear view of the resulting transportation spend and overall network cost."
